=== Git Aliases
tag::concept[]

(((aliases)))
Before we move on to branching, there’s one feature that can make Nora’s life on the command line much easier: **aliases**.

Git doesn't automatically infer your command if you type it in partially. If you don't want to type the entire text of each Git command, you can set up a shorthand (alias) for each command using `git config`.

==== Basic Shorthands
tag::procedure[]

Nora sets up a few aliases for the commands she uses most frequently:

[source,bash]
----
$ git config --global alias.st status
$ git config --global alias.co checkout
$ git config --global alias.ci commit
$ git config --global alias.br branch
----

This means that instead of typing `git status`, Nora can now just type `git st`. For a designer who is constantly checking the state of her project, this saves dozens of keystrokes a day.

==== Creating Custom Commands
tag::procedure[]

Aliases can also be used to create new commands that you think *should* exist. For example, to simplify the process of unstaging a file, Nora adds an `unstage` alias:

[source,bash]
----
$ git config --global alias.unstage 'restore --staged --'
----

Now, these two commands are equivalent:

[source,bash]
----
$ git unstage research/personas/early-adopter.md
$ git restore --staged -- research/personas/early-adopter.md
----

The alias feels much more intuitive. She also creates a `last` command to quickly see her most recent checkpoint:

[source,bash]
----
$ git config --global alias.last 'log -1 HEAD'
----

==== Running External Tools
tag::procedure[]

You can even create aliases that run external shell commands by starting the command with a `!` character. This is useful if Nora has a custom script that generates a design report from her Git history:

[source,bash]
----
$ git config --global alias.report '!./scripts/generate-design-report.sh'
----

Aliases are a great way to customize Git so it speaks your language and fits your personal design workflow.
